FHRMS Frontend – Cursor Rules
1. Project Context (Very Important)

This is a frontend-only EHR application

Backend logic, database design, and security rules are out of scope

The UI must be clinical, professional, and accessible

Target users: doctors, nurses, pharmacists, records officers, admins

Cursor must never invent backend logic.

2. Tech Stack Rules (Strict)

Framework: Next.js (App Router)

Language: TypeScript only

Styling: Tailwind CSS v4

UI components: shadcn/ui

Icons: lucide-react

Linting: ESLint

No CSS-in-JS libraries

No inline styles unless absolutely necessary

❌ Do NOT introduce:

styled-components

Material UI

Chakra UI

React Icons

Font Awesome

3. Next.js Rules

Use App Router (/app) only

Prefer Server Components by default

Use "use client" only when required:

forms

modals

interactive tables

No deprecated pages/ patterns

4. TypeScript Rules (Strict)

No any

No unknown without proper narrowing

Always define explicit types for:

props

API responses

state

Prefer type over interface unless extension is required

Example rule:

If a type is unclear, define a new type — do not guess.

5. Component Design Rules

Components must be:

Small

Reusable

Single-responsibility

Structure:

components/
  ui/        → shadcn components
  layout/    → Sidebar, Topbar
  patients/
  encounters/


❌ No god-components
❌ No 500-line components

6. Styling Rules (Tailwind 4)

Use Tailwind utility classes only

No custom CSS unless unavoidable

Prefer design tokens:

spacing

typography

colors

Follow clinical readability standards:

Body text: 16px

H1: 24–28px

Minimum text size: 14px

❌ No tiny text
❌ No flashy gradients
❌ No playful colors

7. shadcn/ui Rules

Use shadcn components as base primitives

Customize via Tailwind classes, not rewrites

Do not duplicate existing shadcn components

Example:

If a Button exists, extend it — don’t recreate it.

8. Icons Rules

Use lucide-react only

Icons must be:

16–20px for buttons

20–24px for sidebar

Icons must always have text labels in navigation

❌ No icon-only navigation for critical actions

9. Accessibility Rules (Mandatory)

All inputs must have labels

Buttons must have visible text or aria-label

Tables must use semantic HTML

Color contrast must be readable

Healthcare UI requires clarity over density.

10. State & Data Rules

This project uses mock data or API placeholders

Cursor must not:

invent Firestore schemas

invent backend endpoints

Use /services for API calls

Use /types for shared types

11. Error Handling & UX

Always include:

loading states

empty states

error states

Never silently fail

Confirm destructive actions

12. Naming Conventions

Components: PascalCase

Files: kebab-case.tsx

Types: PascalCase

Variables: camelCase

No abbreviations that reduce clarity.

13. Code Quality Rules

Prefer clarity over cleverness

No unnecessary abstractions

No premature optimization

Comments only when logic is non-obvious

Cursor should generate readable human code, not AI-looking code.

14. Domain Rules (FHRMS-Specific)

Use healthcare terminology correctly:

Patient (not user)

Encounter (not visit record)

Clinician (not doctor everywhere)

Never hardcode medical values

Federated Patient ID is read-only in UI

15. Final Rule (Very Important)

If unsure, ask for clarification instead of guessing.

Cursor must not invent requirements.

Project-specific UI rules:

- STRICTLY use the existing color variables defined in the project.
- Never introduce new colors, hex values, or Tailwind color utilities.
- Always reference colors via the existing CSS variables or Tailwind tokens.
- Do not modify the color system unless explicitly instructed.

Component refactoring rules:
- Prefer shadcn/ui components over custom components where possible.
- When converting components, preserve existing layout, spacing, and colors.
- Do not break existing props or public APIs unless necessary.

Dashboard rules:
- The dashboard is the design and behavior reference for all other pages.
- All future pages must follow the same layout structure, spacing scale, and component patterns.
- Fix unfinished or broken behaviors (buttons, tabs, dropdowns, modals, charts).

Behavior rules:
- Ensure all UI interactions work as expected.
- No dead UI elements (every button must have a purpose or handler).
- Add missing state and handlers where behavior is implied.

Output rules:
- Make incremental changes, not full rewrites.
- Do not remove working features.
- Prioritize correctness and consistency over creativity.
